#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.14"
# dependencies = []
# ///
"""Name a tmux session after an agent session.

Only an agent tmux itself was told to run names anything: the pane must
have been started with an agent launcher, so an agent typed at a shell
prompt leaves that session's name alone. The name describes what tmux
was opened to do, not whatever is running in it at the moment.

Among the agents that qualify, the first to name a session owns it
until the session ends, and one started inside another's pane leaves
the name alone. A session carries one name, so an agent launched as the
command of a second pane still moves that name, now through the owner's
slot and format. To hand a session to a different agent, unset its
owner with `tmux set-option -u @agent_namer` and start that agent as a
pane's own command, since the prompt route is refused either way.

A pi or opencode run takes the session name as its argument and
renames once per run: pi's pane title carries the working directory,
and opencode's is prefixed and truncated, so pi's extension and
opencode's plugin each hand the current name over at session start
and after every rename. An empty name renames to the bare agent and
slot -- the form a session keeps until a real name exists, which is
how pi and opencode sessions first name themselves.

A muse-run run supervises a Muse session the tmuse way: tmux starts
this helper as the pane command, the helper forks a watcher child,
and the parent execs `muse`, so the pane process itself is the agent.
The watcher maps the pane to its Muse session through the session
log's route facts, renaming the session to the current name, and
remembering it for the run. It runs beside any agent sandbox rather
than inside it, so watching needs no tool call nor an approval.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import functools
import json
import os
import re
import select
import shlex
import signal
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from enum import StrEnum
from typing import IO, cast

type JsonObject = dict[str, object]

CODEX_GLOBAL_TITLE_WARNING = (
